hi there Steve Kaufman here so today I'm  going to start answering some of the  questions that I received when we had  our little competition tell me what you  would like to talk to Steve Kaufman  about if you had a 30-minute Skype  session let me say as well that we're  trying to schedule this online session  with our winner Daniel Candido I think  his name was so  Jurina of our office is gonna try to set  that up it won't be next week because  I'm busy with visitors but we'll be  doing that but in the meantime I thought  I would try and address some of the  other questions that we receive and I'm  gonna do them in no particular order our  offices put them together in a list and  I'll simply go through them and if you  have additional questions please don't  hesitate to send them along so actually  I can see this if I want a coaching  session with Steve I would ask advice on  how to improve my English from  intermediate to to advanced okay first  of all the difference between  intermediate to an advanced is sometimes  difficult to define when we are fairly  advanced in a language like intermediate  2 we are often more conscious of our  shortcomings than the people were  talking to who think we speak very well  and we feel that there's stuff that we  didn't understand or things that we had  trouble saying so that it's a very fine  line so that's point number one  so don't be too harsh on yourself point  number two the only way you can improve  in a language whatever your level is to  continue to engage with the language and  to challenge yourself so if you're an  advanced or at least intermediate to you  aren't improving English you have to  read more and more challenging material  you have to read novels you have to read  you know do as much reading as you can  listening  you can and eventually of course  speaking in order to speak well you have  to speak a lot you know I am a great  proponent of input based learning and  for example in my Arabic I'm not going  to bother trying to speak until I have  at least 3,000 words but I know that  eventually in order to speak well I have  to speak a lot those languages that I  speak the best are the languages that  I've spoken the most but also the  languages where I have read the most and  listened the most so now on the positive  side when you are at the intermediate  two level you are now at a level where  the language is enjoyable you can in  fact read on a variety of subjects watch  movies engage with people you can do all  these things that are you know going to  improve your language and you can do  them as part of your slow process of  improvement so in summary don't be too  hard on yourself you're probably better  than you think and the only way you can  move along to reach a more advanced  level is to read more listen more and  speak more and write more so just keep  on doing more of the same that would be  my answer to that question second  question was how do I prove my listening  abilities in Japanese I've been studying  for eight years I often have this  question about listening comprehension  listening comprehension is in some ways  the most difficult skill and I remember  living in Japan I was doing business in  Japanese I was reading a newspaper in  Japanese but when I was watching  Japanese movies lots of it I still  couldn't understand it just takes a long  time for the brain to get used to this  new language and I think the only way to  improve our listening ability is to  listen but I find that when I listen in  languages where I'm fairly advanced and  I keep on missing certain parts I have  to I want to have the text I want to  listen to content where I can also read  and look up the words and of course I'm  using link I save the words and I might  see them again  in other words you have to as I say so  much in language learning is a matter of  training the brain so if you just let  the language go off in the background  and you're not sort of zooming in on  those phrases that you have trouble  picking up maybe you won't get them you  might get them on the third or fourth or  fifth time but you may not so therefore  I think it you should make it a practice  for example if you're you know very  advanced in Japanese get yourself an  audio book I know there are audio books  in Japanese I have some and in fact  there are audio books of you know 90s or  early twentieth century literature I  have some there are also political you  know the NHK takes I don't know if the  texts are available you have to go out  there and find material where you have  both the audio and the text and then  listen to it and then read it and if  you're using links save words and  phrases so that you gradually train your  brain it's a gradual process it's not a  matter of a while I give up I'll never  get any better  you have to deliberately work it work at  it next question here I would ask  another burden otherwise more  efficiently and how to improve my  English even well I think this is more  of the same whatever your level you have  to put that time and effort into it  there's no and it is a slow process and  maybe one more how would I best way to  transfer reading skills into speaking  skills in Chinese I mean it's all the  same all of us we may be more advanced  in some skills than other skills so if  some people in fact are better at spoken  Chinese and nada written Chinese but  then you have to put more effort into  those skills that are lacking  speaking in Chinese conversation Chinese  is difficult because Chinese has  somewhat fewer sounds it has the tones  and also unfortunately Chinese people  from different parts of China pronounce  Chinese in different ways so you may  find it easy to speak to someone from  Donbas and you may not understand  someone from Shanghai or from even from  Beijing who may be speaking something  very close to Mandarin so there again  what can I say you know continue your  reading do a lot of listening but see if  you can find people with whom to speak  to speak well you have to speak a lot  you have there is no there is no  shortcut you can't go from just  listening and reading and bingo all of a  sudden you can speak it doesn't work  that way you build up your basic  capability in the language your  vocabulary your ability to comprehend  your sense of the language but then you  have to activate it and to activate it  you have to speak and you have to speak  a lot all right and maybe that last one  here how to work on my pronunciation now  okay pronunciation first of all let me  say that I have interacted with  non-native speakers of English who spoke  English extremely well who used words  well who express themselves with  eloquence and had a fairly strong accent  and it never bothered me  pronunciation only becomes an issue if  people can't understand you when people  hear an accent but they hear someone who  speaks very well I think they're almost  more impressed I love listening to  people with a French accent or a  Japanese accent or whatever who just  used the language well so your first  task is to use the language well and to  understand well because there's nothing  worse than talking to someone who seems  like not to understand what you're  saying now next question the  pronounciation itself I've always said  the most important thing is intonation  if you can get the intonation of the  language right a lot of the  pronunciation the individual sounds will  fall into place also if you get the  intonation right that's very pleasing to  the ear of the native speaker it's more  important in my view than the individual  vowel sounds or consonant sound that you  might pronounce somewhat more you know  in a French manner or a Spanish manner  Japanese or Chinese or Russian matter  that's less important get the intonation  right so how do you work on that  well if I were doing it if I were  deliberately working on I tend not to  work on pronunciation because I listens  to so much that and I don't try to speak  at an early stage so that I listen to so  much like I've been listening to so much  Arabic in Arabic is bouncing around in  my brain when I eventually go to speak  it I'll have a bit of a head start on  the pronunciation because I've been  doing so much listening but if at a  certain point I wanted to improve I  would take a short initially a sentence  or a paragraph at link and I would  listen to it listen to the voice and  then I would try to read it or even from  memory say it focusing entirely on the  rhythm and the intonation of the  language and I would just work on  getting that intonation right because  with the intonation for example in  Chinese if you get the intonation your  tones your individual word tones are  going to improve rather than trying to  focus on say the tone of an individual  syllable or the pronunciation of an  individual word if you can get the  intonation of the phrases and the  sentences I think the rest of your  pronunciation is going to improve  so my advice on pronunciation is hey  don't worry about it too much be focus  on intonation the intonation of phrases  and sentences and you can do that by  having a text in front of you listening  to it and then reading it or going from  memory and then try and imitate the  intonation and you may want to record  yourself and compare yourself to the  native speaker if you if you  mispronounce the artwork don't worry  about it focus just on get the  intonation okay that would be my advice  and with that I've handled a few of  these questions and I will go through  the rest of them and there's quite a lot  of them and if you have additional  questions please don't hesitate and if  you like these videos please subscribe  so bye for now  [Music]
 

